        "bg": "palette--light bg--neutral"
      },
      "blocks": {
        "faq_q1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How often should I use purple shampoo on gray hair?",
            "content": "<p>Most gray hair types benefit from using a purple or silver shampoo two to three times per week. If your grays are particularly porous or prone to brassiness, you can use it every wash — just monitor for any lavender cast, which signals you should dial back frequency or reduce leave-in time.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_q2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What is the difference between purple shampoo and a color-depositing mask for gray hair?",
            "content": "<p>Purple shampoo provides a light toning effect during your regular wash routine. A color-depositing mask delivers a more concentrated boost of pigment while simultaneously conditioning the hair. If your gray has strong brassiness or you want a more vivid, intentional silver tone, a mask used once or twice a week will give you a more noticeable result than shampoo alone. In this guide, purple toners are ranked in section 1 and color-depositing masks in section 2 — start with your primary goal: brass control or color payoff.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_q3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What is gray blending and is it better than full coverage color?",
            "content": "<p>Gray blending is a salon technique that uses highlights and lowlights to integrate gray strands with your natural hair color rather than covering them completely. It grows out more naturally than solid dye, with no harsh regrowth line. It is a better option for anyone who wants a low-maintenance transition rather than committing to regular root touch-ups.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_q4": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What is Davines View and how is it different from permanent hair color?",
            "content": "<p>Davines View is a demi-permanent hair gloss with an acidic pH. Unlike permanent color, it does not fully penetrate the hair cortex — it sits on the surface to add tone, reflection, and shine without lifting or depositing heavy pigment. It fades gradually and naturally, making it ideal for gray blending without the harsh regrowth typical of permanent dye.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_q5": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Why does gray hair turn yellow or brassy?",
            "content": "<p>Gray and white hair is naturally more porous than pigmented hair, which means it readily absorbs warm tones from hard water minerals, heat styling tools, UV rays, and environmental pollution. Because there is no underlying pigment to counteract these deposits, they sit visibly on the hair shaft — creating the yellow or brassy tinge that purple-toned products are designed to neutralize.</p>"
          }
        },
        "faq_q6": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I use purple shampoo if I'm trying to keep my grays covered?",
            "content": "<p>If you are covering grays with brunette or dark tones, a purple shampoo is not the right tool — it is designed to enhance cool and silver tones. Instead, opt for a color-depositing product in a warm or neutral brunette shade, like the Davines Alchemic Chocolate Shampoo &amp; Conditioner or the Wella Professionals Color Fresh Mask in Chocolate Touch, to keep coverage looking fresh between salon appointments. See the Temporary Gray Camouflage section above for our two ranked picks — the <a href=\"/products/davines-alchemic-chocolate-shampoo-conditioner\">Davines Alchemic Chocolate Shampoo &amp; Conditioner</a> and the <a href=\"/products/wella-professionals-color-fresh-mask-chocolate-touch\">Wella Color Fresh Mask in Chocolate Touch</a>.</p>\n<p>"
          }
        }
      },
